WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.637
Last, retiring AD CS, the sixth wave.

2
00:00:03.637 --> 00:00:08.727
It applies only to CAs that no longer
serve a retained use; domain controllers

3
00:00:08.727 --> 00:00:13.101
usually keep at least one issuing
CA alive for as long as they exist.

4
00:00:13.101 --> 00:00:17.274
The gate is a finished cutover, a
clean thirty-day rollback window,

5
00:00:17.274 --> 00:00:20.675
and every retained use running
on the CAs that remain.

6
00:00:20.675 --> 00:00:24.999
Then confirm nothing depends on the
CA, stop new issuance, back it up,

7
00:00:24.999 --> 00:00:29.087
publish a final revocation list,
and clean up trust everywhere.

8
00:00:29.087 --> 00:00:33.525
Keep the published endpoints serving
until the last certificate expires.

9
00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:39.113
The assessor reads the result
as NIST SP 800-53 evidence.

10
00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:45.023
So, from Monday: harden first, choose
the root deliberately, issue strongly

11
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:51.225
mapped certificates, keep CRLs reachable,
and retire AD CS last, with evidence.

12
00:00:51.225 --> 00:00:52.060
Thank you.
